[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
so nobody read the bodies. Read by tenant, booking form and body, its reports are three problems
with three different fixes. Each has its own ticket now.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one known issue. Engineering closed it on 5 October with the parser fixed and booking-v3
retired. That covers two of the three problems; the one-day move is still open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} />

## Where each one stands

| problem | ticket | outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `parser` | <a href={issues.parser}>#1</a> | fixed: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` · closed, completed |
| `hint` | <a href={issues.hint}>#2</a> | won't fix: booking-v3 form retired · closed, not planned |
| `one-day` | <a href={issues.oneDay}>#3</a> | open: neither change touches a date that moves by one day |

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads every date month first.** Tenants whose locale writes the day
first get the day and month swapped: 7 April goes out as 4 July. Every channel, every day-first
tenant. Fix: parse in the tenant's locale.

**`hint`: booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field.** Only reports that come in through
the form of a day-first tenant on booking-v3. People type what the hint says, month first, and the
form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The parser fix does not touch this. Fix: print the hint
from the tenant's locale.

**`one-day`: the date moves by one day, not a swap.** Tuesday shows as Monday; one meeting is Tuesday
in Lisbon and Monday in Toronto; an invite arrives before the booking was made. These look like a
timezone or midnight-UTC bug. The desk had already read them this way (`desk-notes.json`); they
stay filed and counted under `parser` by tenant, so this row overlaps the first.

## How to count

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- Count a report where the desk files it, by cause. A one-day move is its own ticket, but the
  desk still counts it under its cause.
- Place a report by its tenant (locale and booking form), not by intake's tag. Intake guesses from
  the reporter's browser and gets it wrong for a tenant whose staff use another locale.
- A subject line says how the reporter felt, not what broke. The same subject sits in all three rows.

